  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies remain partly cloudy at this time\nas residual low-level moisture remains stubborn. Winds are still\nbreezy in the downslope areas of West Wendover and lee-side of\nmountain ranges but should continue to relax as the day wears on.\nFor the rest of today and tonight, look for a shortwave ridge axis\nto pass over the forecast area, bringing quiet conditions to \nnorthern and central Nevada tonight. Overnight low temperatures \nwill dip into the 20s to low 30s.\n\nBy Sunday, all models are showing upper level low pressure off the\nwest coast of the United States. The previously mentioned ridge\nwill be shifting well off to the east. Southwest flow will\ncommence over the forecast area with afternoon high temperatures\nclimbing into the 50s and 60s. No precipitation is expected in \nthe morning. Model simulations are bringing the upper trough onto\nthe coast of California late in the day Sunday in California. This\nsystem is expected to slowly track east into California during \nthe overnight period, with light showery precipitation beginning \nacross western and central Humboldt county Monday morning. \nOvernight lows will be in the 30s. The upper trough will continue \nits slow eastward trek during the Monday timeframe. Strong winds \nare expected to develop Monday afternoon, especially across \ncentral Nevada. South to southwest winds 15 to 25 mph with gusts \n35 to 40 mph are expected mainly across central Nevada during the \nafternoon. Highs on Monday will be in the 40s and 50s. Snow levels\nwill start out rather high at 7000 feet and above during the \nafternoon. On Monday night and Tuesday, the main trough axis is \nexpected to cross western California with a cold front passing \nthrough the state during this time. Overnight lows will be in the \n20s. Snow levels will eventually drop to around 4500 to 5500 feet \nby Tuesday morning. All told, many valley locations will see a \ndusting to an inch in the north, with about an inch or two in \ncentral Nevada. Over the higher passes and summits along roadways \nin the central parts, about one to four inches. Finally, across \nthe higher passes and summits in the north, up to two inches are \npossible. The rain and snow will be slow to clear during the \nTuesday timeframe with near normal daytime highs in the 40s. Winds\nwill be light. \n\nA shortwave ridge will bring quiet conditions but it will not last\nlong. On its heels is the next in a series of systems that will\naffect northern and central Nevada. This system will push across \nthe forecast area during the mid to latter portions of next week. \nThis is expected to be a much colder system and the area could see\nthe snow levels dropping to the valley floors with minor \naccumulations possible. Will need to continue monitoring this \nsystem for possible winter headline products. Highs will generally\nbe in the 30s and 40s with lows in the teens and 20s. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Minor edits were made to the\nshort term wind grids to bump up sustained wind speeds and wind \ngusts in central Nevada on Monday. Confidence is high with respect\nto the dry weather through tomorrow. Confidence remains moderate\nin the next system affecting the area late Sunday through Tuesday.\nConfidence in the third system is low at this time due to\ndifferences in strength and timing.",
